2026-08-22T03:20Z  gap_diagnosis.py: the gap decomposes into TWO independent,
  separately-quantified mechanisms.
  (G1) SS6 bounds (r/n)eps^h_{r-1} by r C_{r-1}(b+1)/n^2, DISCARDING the 1/n.
       Since the theorem's own hypothesis is n >= b+r+1, that factor is worth
       r/n <= r/(b+r+1) < 1.  Keeping it (plus noting (n-1-a-b-r)/n in [0,1], so
       the "2" in front of D_r(b+1) can be 1) is a ONE-LINE rigorous fix and
       turns FACTORIAL into EXPONENTIAL: D'_r(0)/D'_{r-1}(0) -> ~1.26.
       At r=30: original 7.09e30, improved 9.9e3, true 11.13.
  (G2) SS4's coefficient-sum norm.  PROVED EXACTLY here: because s->1-s makes all
       contributions to a given coefficient share the sign (-1)^j,
           ||F_r(1-.,b)|| = F_r(2,b),  ||G_r(1-.,b)|| = G_r(2,b),
           ||Hhat_r(.,b)|| = 2 F_r(2,b+1)      (r=0..24, b=0..3, exact)
       and F_r(2,0)/F_r(1,0) = Theta((9/8)^r) -- ratio-in-r measured 1.125012 at
       r=60, and F_r(2,0)/[phi_r (9/8)^r] -> 1.5.  Mechanism: F_r(2,0) =
       (phi_r/4^r) sum_{i<=r} 2^{r-i}C(2r+1,i) whose summand peaks at i=(2r-1)/3,
       INSIDE the summation range, so the restricted sum is already the full
       (3/2)^{2r+1}2^r.  So each use of the coefficient-sum norm costs (9/8)^r.

